WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:06.980
"ApoE—The Single Most Important
Gene for Longevity"

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.600
Though the genetic
contribution to lifespan

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.100
may be relatively minor,

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.720
are there specific genes

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.060
that have been associated
with longevity?

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.460
The leading method
for complex genetic mapping

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.040
is called genome-wide
association analysis,

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:23.040
which is essentially
a massive game of Go Fish,

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.500
comparing a million or more
letters of DNA

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.820
between groups
of similar people,

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.200
looking for a match.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:29.220
So for example,

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.640
if you stretch out the DNA
of hundreds of centenarians

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.820
and compare those sequences
to that of noncentenarians,

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.400
is there a DNA letter
at a certain position

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.860
that centenarians share
disproportionately?

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.460
The problem is
hundreds of centenarians

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.980
are all researchers are
typically able to find.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.220
Extremely long-lived
individuals,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:51.300
such as centenarians,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.640
comprise only a tiny fraction
of the population,

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.040
on the order of
1 in 10,000 or so.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.420
As you can imagine,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:01.200
the more people you have in
a genome-wide association study,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.940
the more likely
you'll be able to find

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.740
a needle in the DNA haystack.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.860
With so few people
available to study

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.480
for extreme longevity research,

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.720
it becomes much more difficult
to identify trends.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.660
Some researchers have tried
solving this problem

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.420
by lowering the age requirement
to 85.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.300
Then you can enroll
thousands into your study,

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.160
but making it to 90

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.820
is not the same
as making it to 100.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.200
In fact, statistically, it's
as hard to get from 90 to 100

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.620
as it is to get to 90
in the first place.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.800
So, by using
younger age brackets,

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.520
we may miss out on discovering
some secret centenarian sauce.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.080
That brings us to the largest
genome-wide association study

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.220
of lifespan to date,

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.620
based on data
from a million people.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.340
How were researchers able
to include so many subjects?

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.920
By correlating
the genetic fingerprints

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.780
of half a million
middle-aged individuals

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.300
with the ages
of both of their parents,

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:01.560
they were able to find

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.740
a dozen DNA regions
linked to lifespan

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.620
that appeared to account for
up to five years' difference

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.760
between individuals.

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.840
Twelve DNA markers is actually
a surprisingly low number,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.760
compared to height,
for example,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.900
which is determined by more than
400 different DNA spots--

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.380
although, unlike lifespan,
height is highly heritable.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.960
A review of all
of the genome-wide

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.120
association studies
for longevity put together

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.940
only found one gene confirmed

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.800
in multiple independent
meta-analyses:

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.680
the "Alzheimer's gene,"
APOE.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:38.700
Beyond just determining
lower or higher dementia risk,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.820
APOE is the single
most important gene

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.320
when it comes to longevity--

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.020
though again, that's not
necessarily saying much.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:51.660
APOE codes for a protein
that is 299 amino acids long

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.640
called apolipoprotein E.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.360
Some people have APOE genes
that code for the protein

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.840
with the amino acid cysteine
at positions 112 and 158,

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.060
known as the APOE2 variant,

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:07.260
while others have
an arginine in those spots--

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.900
the APOE4 variant
of the protein,

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:12.780
and APOE3 is the third major
type of variant,

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.100
which has one of each.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.700
Having genes that code
for the APOE4 variant

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.920
increases your risk
of cognitive decline,

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.700
full-blown Alzheimer's disease,
and premature death.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.360
If you have one copy
of the APOE4 gene

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:28.260
from your mother or father,

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:29.820
your odds of becoming
a centenarian

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.260
are cut roughly in half,

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.200
and if you have APOE4 genes
from both parents,

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.740
your centenarian odds drop
by more than 80 percent.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.260
What does this protein do
to have such a powerful impact

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:44.820
on our health and longevity?

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.320
APOE is the primary cholesterol
carrier in the brain

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.600
and plays a major role
packaging and transporting

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.340
LDL cholesterol
throughout the body.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.300
The LDL cholesterol level
in those with APOE4 genes

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.100
averages more than
40 points higher

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.720
than those with APOE2 genes,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.260
gunking up the arteries that
feed both the heart and brain.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.980
LDL cholesterol is a risk factor
not just for heart disease

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.720
but Alzheimer's disease
as well.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.780
Switch people to a diet
lower in animal fat

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:13.620
and cholesterol, though,

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.620
and those LDL differences
can disappear,

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.560
nearly a 60-point drop in LDL.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.060
The difference
in cholesterol level

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.980
caused by
the different APOE genes

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.660
can simply disappear

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.200
if you eat a diet low enough in
saturated fat and cholesterol.

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.080
So diet can trump genetics.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.860
This may explain
the so-called Nigerian paradox.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.560
If you inherit one APOE4 gene,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.450
your risk of getting Alzheimer's
may triple,

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.240
and if you get APOE4 genes
from both parents--

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.640
which occurs in about one
in fifty people in the US--

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.560
you might end up
with nine times the risk.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:49.980
The highest frequency
of the APOE4 variant

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:51.480
occurs in Nigerians,

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:53.880
but they also have
some of the lowest rates

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:54.960
of Alzheimer's disease.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:56.280
How can that be?

00:04:56.280 --> 00:05:00.060
The population with the highest
rate of the "Alzheimer's gene"

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.700
has one of the lowest rates
of Alzheimer's disease?

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.880
This contradiction
may be explained

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.940
by Nigerians' extremely low
blood-cholesterol levels,

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:10.560
thanks to a diet
low in animal fat,

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.500
and consisting mainly
of grains and vegetables.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:15.480
Humans may have evolved

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:18.300
to maintain an LDL level
of about 25,

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.460
but the average
in the Western world

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:22.680
is approximately 120.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:25.920
It's no wonder heart disease
is the leading cause of death

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.480
in high-income countries,

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:28.920
and Alzheimer's disease,

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:30.420
according to
the World Health Organization,

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:31.860
is killer #2.

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:37.560
Too often, doctors and patients
have a fatalistic approach

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.120
to chronic
degenerative diseases,

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.920
and Alzheimer's is no exception.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:43.560
"It's all in your genes,"
they say,

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.480
"and what will happen
will happen."

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:49.260
But research shows that although
you might have been dealt

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.940
some poor genetic cards,

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.020
you may be able
to reshuffle the deck with diet.

